  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on self-reported household data from the Household Pulse Survey to measure program participation stages rather than administrative registry data from the ERA programs themselves.",
      "what": "This study merged household microdata from the U.S. Census Bureau's Household Pulse Survey with state-level Emergency Rental Assistance (ERA) program data to analyze how policy variations influenced program participation. Using sequential logit models, it tracked renter households through the application, decision, and receipt stages of the $47 billion federal program. The analysis found that policies designed to lower administrative burdens, such as allowing self-attestation or using proxy documentation, significantly increased the likelihood of low-income households applying for and receiving financial aid.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that reducing administrative barriers and prioritizing vulnerable groups directly increases the take-up of emergency social safety net benefits, providing empirical evidence to guide the design of post-pandemic housing assistance programs."
    }
